1
00:00:00,375 --> 00:00:02,725
Your alarm clock is the cold.

2
00:00:02,725 --> 00:00:07,762
Welcome to an imagined summer day in
Europe, about twenty thousand years
ago.

3
00:00:07,762 --> 00:00:11,388
You are a hunter-gatherer, and your
first job is keeping yesterday's

4
00:00:11,388 --> 00:00:12,838
embers alive.

5
00:00:12,838 --> 00:00:17,250
A few dry twigs, a careful breath, and
breakfast has a chance.

6
00:00:18,025 --> 00:00:20,713
Stone Age does not mean simple-minded.

7
00:00:20,713 --> 00:00:23,038
A sharp flake takes skill to make.

8
00:00:23,038 --> 00:00:25,363
A scraper helps prepare hides.

9
00:00:25,363 --> 00:00:29,162
A bone needle can help turn those hides
into warmer clothing.

10
00:00:29,162 --> 00:00:32,000
Break your favorite tool, and there is
no store.

11
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:33,500
There is practice.

12
00:00:34,250 --> 00:00:38,038
Before heading out, your group checks
equipment and plans a route.

13
00:00:38,038 --> 00:00:39,225
Where is water?

14
00:00:39,225 --> 00:00:40,900
Where were animals seen?

15
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:42,512
Who stays near camp?

16
00:00:42,512 --> 00:00:46,425
The details of this little group are
invented, but surviving together is

17
00:00:46,425 --> 00:00:47,450
the point.

18
00:00:47,450 --> 00:00:51,025
Nobody here is a one-person survival
channel.

19
00:00:51,825 --> 00:00:54,438
Finding food means more than hunting.

20
00:00:54,438 --> 00:00:58,675
Depending on the place and season,
edible plants can matter too.

21
00:00:58,675 --> 00:01:01,262
Knowing what to collect is learned
knowledge.

22
00:01:01,262 --> 00:01:03,262
You watch someone experienced.

23
00:01:03,262 --> 00:01:05,012
Confidence is useful.

24
00:01:05,012 --> 00:01:09,500
Confidently eating an unfamiliar plant
is a much worse plan.

25
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:12,887
Farther out, tracks tell another story.

26
00:01:12,887 --> 00:01:16,862
Hunters read the ground, watch the
wind, and look for animals.

27
00:01:16,862 --> 00:01:21,038
A distant reindeer is a possibility,
not a packed lunch.

28
00:01:21,038 --> 00:01:24,337
Getting close enough takes patience and
cooperation.

29
00:01:24,337 --> 00:01:27,175
Your spear does not come with a
guarantee.

30
00:01:27,925 --> 00:01:31,437
Today, in our imagined story, the herd
moves away.

31
00:01:31,437 --> 00:01:34,062
The hunters return without a fresh
kill.

32
00:01:34,062 --> 00:01:36,587
That is not the end of everyone's work.

33
00:01:36,587 --> 00:01:41,050
People gather fuel, sort what was
collected, and share tasks.

34
00:01:41,050 --> 00:01:45,250
One disappointing trip is easier to
handle when you have other people.

35
00:01:46,025 --> 00:01:48,337
Back at camp, the quiet jobs matter.

36
00:01:48,337 --> 00:01:50,087
A hide needs scraping.

37
00:01:50,087 --> 00:01:51,962
A sleeve needs stitching.

38
00:01:51,962 --> 00:01:53,812
Firewood needs collecting.

39
00:01:53,812 --> 00:01:55,562
Food needs preparing.

40
00:01:55,562 --> 00:01:59,225
You spend part of the afternoon
repairing the things that keep you warm

41
00:01:59,225 --> 00:02:00,288
and fed.

42
00:02:00,288 --> 00:02:03,950
Apparently, chores are older than your
excuses.

43
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:07,362
By evening, people gather around the
fire.

44
00:02:07,362 --> 00:02:10,200
They eat, watch, and learn from one
another.

45
00:02:10,200 --> 00:02:14,087
We cannot recover the exact
conversations of a day like this.

46
00:02:14,087 --> 00:02:18,175
But tools, food remains, and hearths
reveal skilled people building

47
00:02:18,175 --> 00:02:19,388
lives together.

48
00:02:19,388 --> 00:02:21,888
Tomorrow brings more uncertainty.

49
00:02:21,888 --> 00:02:25,550
Tonight, you have warmth, company, and
a place to sleep.
